 "text": "The We Can Do It poster wasn't famous during World War II. Almost nobody saw it. 1943. Artist J. Howard Miller makes it for Westinghouse, a company with factories supplying the war. The goal? Keep workers motivated. It hangs in a few of their factories. For just two weeks. Then it disappears. At the time, America's Rosie the Riveter was someone else. a hit song, and a famous Norman Rockwell painting. Then, in the 1980s, the old poster is rediscovered. Copies spread everywhere. People start calling her Rosie the Riveter. She becomes a symbol of women's strength. A poster almost nobody saw became one of the most famous images in American history. Every legend has a beginning.",
